13
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:57,060
Diagram labeling is a common question type in the IELTS listening test,

14
00:00:57,480 --> 00:00:58,920
especially in section two.

15
00:00:59,620 --> 00:01:04,200
However, many candidates find it confusing and often lose points when they

16
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:05,100
encounter this question.

17
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:10,000
In this question type, you're required to complete a diagram that describes a

18
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,100
process, object, structure, machine, or natural phenomena, for example,

19
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:16,860
fossils.

20
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:19,380
There are two types of labeling a diagram.

21
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:24,660
Format one, listen to the recording and fill the correct information in the

22
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:25,160
blanks.

23
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:29,880
Format two, you are given a list of words that need filling in and are

24
00:01:29,880 --> 00:01:32,420
labeled as A, B, C, and D.

25
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:37,360
Then you're required to choose and write the corresponding letters in the

26
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:37,720
diagram.

27
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:43,220
These questions are usually not too challenging because the diagram provides

28
00:01:43,220 --> 00:01:45,100
much given information already.

29
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:49,660
This makes it easier for candidates to follow along during the listening test.

30
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:54,580
So what's the most effective strategy for this question type?

31
00:01:55,420 --> 00:02:00,060
Let's move on to the second part, strategy or steps to handle the question

32
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:00,380
type.

33
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:06,500
Let's take a look at this question.

34
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:09,419
For this question, here's what we're going to do.

35
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:12,540
Step one, read the instructions carefully.

36
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:17,880
To note the word limit, this listening activity provides us with a list of

37
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:18,240
words.

38
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:22,220
So we don't need to worry about the word limit, but careful, make sure when

39
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:25,480
you fill in the answers that you write the letter rather than the word.

40
00:02:26,180 --> 00:02:31,740
Step two, read the title of the diagram and given information, then underline

41
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:32,360
keywords.

42
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:35,340
During this step, we can also predict the answer.

43
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,300
This diagram has the title media headset, meaning we can expect to hear about

44
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:44,140
the different parts of a headset and their functions.

45
00:02:44,440 --> 00:02:48,640
We can also see that many of the words in our word list are nouns,

46
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:51,120
different parts of the headset itself.

47
00:02:52,420 --> 00:02:56,560
Step three, listen to the recording and choose the correct answer.

48
00:02:57,100 --> 00:03:01,220
While listening, we need to note down information right on the diagram.

49
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:03,140
OK, ready to listen?

50
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:04,080
Let's get started.

51
00:03:08,970 --> 00:03:13,610
You will hear a technician named Mark giving instructions about the parts and

52
00:03:13,610 --> 00:03:17,510
functions of a media headset at a modern media centre.

53
00:03:18,330 --> 00:03:22,610
First you have some time to look at questions 11 to 16.

54
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:58,380
Now listen and answer questions 11 to 16.

55
00:03:59,220 --> 00:04:03,340
Good morning everyone and welcome to our modern media centre.

56
00:04:03,660 --> 00:04:06,540
My name is Mark and I am the technician here.

57
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:11,860
Today we are going to learn about a device that we use every single day

58
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:15,500
in our radio and recording studios, the media headset.

59
00:04:16,579 --> 00:04:21,280
Now please look at the diagram of the media headset in your information packs.

60
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:23,780
Let's identify the parts together.

61
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:27,660
First look at the very top of the device.

62
00:04:27,660 --> 00:04:32,240
This is the curved part that sits on top of your head to hold the

63
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:33,180
device in place.

64
00:04:33,860 --> 00:04:35,440
We call this the band.

65
00:04:36,340 --> 00:04:41,000
On our models, this is padded with soft foam so that it doesn't hurt your

66
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:42,960
head during long recording sessions.

67
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:46,560
And it is adjustable, so it can fit anyone comfortably.

68
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:50,800
Now look at the circular parts that cover your ears.

69
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:55,540
To make sure the sound from the room doesn't get into your ears, we use

70
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:58,140
a soft round pad on each side.

71
00:04:58,700 --> 00:05:03,780
These are very important for noise cancelling, which helps you focus only on

72
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:05,120
the audio you are recording.

73
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:10,300
Attached to the left side of the headset is a long flexible arm.

74
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:12,760
At the end of this arm is the microphone.

75
00:05:13,460 --> 00:05:15,380
This is the part you speak into.

76
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:20,420
You should always position this about 2 cm away from your mouth.

77
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:23,640
If it is too close, your voice will be too loud.

78
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:27,160
If it is too far, we won't be able to hear you at all.

79
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:33,020
This headset is not a Bluetooth connection one, so it has a cable which helps

80
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:36,700
you connect with other devices, such as phones or laptops.

81
00:05:37,380 --> 00:05:41,760
This is about 2 meters long, which gives you plenty of space to move around

82
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:43,320
your desk while you are working.

83
00:05:43,960 --> 00:05:48,340
If you look a bit down to the cable, you will see a small plastic

84
00:05:48,340 --> 00:05:50,140
box with a sliding button.

85
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:51,940
This is a switch.

86
00:05:51,940 --> 00:05:57,180
We use this to quickly turn the microphone on or off, or to change the

87
00:05:57,180 --> 00:05:58,860
volume of the music you are hearing.

88
00:05:59,660 --> 00:06:04,220
Finally, at the very end of the cord, you will see the metal tip.

89
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:08,200
This is the part you connect to the computer or the recording machine.

90
00:06:08,780 --> 00:06:10,440
This is what we call the plug.

91
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,320
What's great about this part is that it is compatible with almost all types of

92
00:06:15,320 --> 00:06:15,660
devices.

93
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:20,660
When connecting, make sure you push it in all the way until you hear a

94
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:21,400
small click.

95
00:06:22,260 --> 00:06:25,140
Does everyone have any questions about...

96
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:27,300
That's the end of part 2.

97
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:31,920
